---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "ensure protein powder",
      "ensure max protein powder",
      "ensure original nutrition powder",
      "ensure max protein 3-in-1 muscle support",
      "is ensure protein powder third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Powders for Seniors",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Ensure Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Ensure now sells two very different powders in the US: a new 30 g protein, creatine and HMB tub, and the classic Original nutrition powder. Here is what each label says, what it costs per 25 g of protein and who should buy which.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Ensure Products Ranked</a>
      <a href="#which-powders">Which Ensure Powders Exist</a>
      <a href="#max-protein-label">Max Protein 3-in-1 Label</a>
      <a href="#original-label">Original Powder Label</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#vs-shakes">Powder vs Shakes vs Premier</a>
      <a href="#health">Seniors, Diabetes and Kidneys</a>
      <a href="#buyers">What Buyers Say</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 Muscle Support is the Ensure powder worth buying for protein: 30 g of protein, 5 g of creatine and 3 g of calcium HMB per serving, with an NSF Contents Certified claim, at roughly $2 to $2.45 per 25 g of protein. Ensure Original powder is a different product, a sugary 9 g protein nutrition drink. This review covers both labels, the testing question, cost, health caveats and buyer feedback.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Ensure Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 Muscle Support is a good protein powder at a fair price for what it contains, and Ensure Original powder is not really a protein powder at all. The 3-in-1 tub gives 30 g of protein plus 5 g of creatine and 3 g of calcium HMB per serving, per Abbott, and works out at about $2.05 to $2.45 per 25 g of protein in November 2026. The Original powder gives 9 g of protein and 13 g of added sugar in 250 calories, which makes it a calorie supplement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy the 3-in-1 if you want a single scoop that covers protein and creatine, you lift weights or are trying to keep muscle as you age, and you like buying from a pharmacy brand you recognise. Skip it if you only want protein, because a plain whey costs less per gram, or if you have kidney disease and have not cleared creatine and a 30 g protein dose with your doctor. Buy the Original powder only when the goal is extra calories, and ideally when a clinician has suggested it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Ensure Powders at a Glance (per label, Nov 2026)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 3-in-1 serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">9 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per Original serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$2.05</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">3-in-1 cost per 25 g protein (Walmart)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">NSF</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Contents Certified (3-in-1 only)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Ensure Powders and the Shake Alternative, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three Ensure powders and the ready-to-drink shake most people compare them with, ranked for someone who wants protein first. Figures are per the brand label or retailer listing at the time of writing (November 2026) and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-powders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Ensure Protein Powders Are Sold in the US Right Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two Ensure powders are sold in the US at the time of writing: Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 Muscle Support, in chocolate and vanilla, and Ensure Original Nutrition Powder, which Abbott lists in vanilla. Everything else on the Ensure shelf, including Ensure Plus, Ensure High Protein, Ensure Complete and the regular Max Protein line, is sold as ready-to-drink bottles in the US. A search for Ensure protein powder at Target in November 2026 returned only bottled shakes, which is why so many shoppers are confused about what an Ensure powder actually is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 3-in-1 powder is new. Abbott announced it on October 7, 2026, describing it as a supplement stack in one scoop: protein to build muscle with resistance training, creatine for recovery, and HMB to slow muscle breakdown. The launch release names Walmart and Amazon as the retailers, and the Abbott Store sells it in two-tub packs. Because it is so new, there is far less buyer feedback than for the bottles, and some listing details, such as the full ingredient panel, were still missing from brand pages when we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ensure Original powder is the old can most people picture. It is a 14.1 oz (397 g) can of vanilla powder that you stir into water to make an 8 fl oz drink. Abbott describes it as complete, balanced nutrition, and the label backs that up in the sense that it carries 24 vitamins and minerals, but the macro profile is mostly carbohydrate. Outside the US, Ensure sells 800 to 850 g tubs of chocolate and vanilla powder in Australia and New Zealand, which are different formulas; do not use their labels for the US can.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want Ensure's 30 g protein in the simplest form, the Max Protein shake in a bottle is still the product to know. We include it in the cards above as the comparison point, and our <a href="/premier-protein-vs-ensure/">Premier Protein vs Ensure comparison</a> covers the bottles in detail. This review stays on the powders and only brings the bottles in where the choice between scoop and bottle matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="max-protein-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per Abbott, each serving of Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 contains 30 g of protein, 5 g of creatine monohydrate and 3 g of calcium HMB. Ensure's vanilla product page lists 150 calories and 2 g of sugar per serving, while one Amazon listing shows 170 calories, most likely the chocolate tub, so expect 150 to 170 depending on flavor. The directions are two scoops in 12 fl oz of liquid once a day, and Abbott says one tub makes 15 shakes. It is labeled gluten-free, kosher and halal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The three actives are sensible doses. Thirty grams of protein sits in the per-meal range that research on older adults most often uses, because older muscle responds less to small servings. Five grams of creatine monohydrate is the standard daily maintenance dose used in most studies, and creatine combined with resistance training has a strong evidence base for strength and lean mass, including in older adults. Three grams of calcium HMB matches the amount Abbott cites for muscle preservation; the evidence for HMB is thinner and more mixed than for creatine, so treat it as a minor extra.
          </p>

          <p>
            What we could not confirm is just as important. At the time of writing, neither Ensure.com nor the Abbott Store published the full ingredient list, the protein source or the sweeteners for the 3-in-1 tub; the nutrition table on both pages was empty. Abbott's Max Protein bottles use milk protein concentrate and caseinates sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, but that is a different product, so do not assume the powder matches. If you avoid sucralose or soy, read the panel on the tub before you open it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two scoops weigh roughly 53 g if a 28 oz tub holds 15 servings, which is our arithmetic rather than a label figure. Of that, 38 g is protein, creatine and HMB, so there is little room for fillers, which is a good sign. If you already take creatine, do not double up: this tub replaces your creatine scoop. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">protein powders with creatine</a> guide compares other combined products, and <a href="/creatine-vs-protein-powder/">creatine vs protein powder</a> explains what each one does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="original-label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Ensure Original Nutrition Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure Original powder is mostly sugar and starch with a small amount of protein. Per Abbott's product page, a 57 g serving, which is half a cup of powder, gives 250 calories, 9 g of protein, 34 g of carbohydrate, 13 g of total and added sugar, 9 g of fat and 0 g of fiber. Several retailer panels and an Ensure FAQ list 240 calories and 8 g of protein instead, which probably reflects an older label version, so check the can you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list starts with corn syrup, corn maltodextrin and sugar, then corn oil, then the proteins: sodium caseinate, soy protein isolate and calcium caseinate, with artificial flavor, soy lecithin and a vitamin and mineral premix. It contains milk and soy. Abbott labels it gluten-free, suitable for lactose intolerance, kosher and halal, and not for people with galactosemia. The micronutrients are the selling point: 30% of the daily value for vitamin D, 15% for iron and a spread of B vitamins and minerals.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max Protein 3-in-1</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Original powder</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">13 g (all added)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Extras</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g creatine, 3 g HMB</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 vitamins and minerals</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings per container</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That profile has a real use. For someone who is losing weight without trying, has a small appetite or is recovering from illness, 250 easy calories with vitamins can matter more than protein. Mixing the powder with milk instead of water, which the label does not require, adds roughly 8 g of protein per cup. But if your goal is muscle, 9 g of protein per serving is a third of what the 3-in-1 gives, and 13 g of added sugar is more than half the daily limit many guidelines suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Abbott's directions are to pour three quarters of a cup of cold water into a glass and gradually stir in half a cup of leveled powder. Once mixed, drink it promptly or refrigerate it covered and use it within 24 hours. An opened can should be kept covered in a cool, dry place and used within three weeks, which is easy at one serving a day but worth noting if it is an occasional drink.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Ensure Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure Max Protein 3-in-1 is, according to Abbott, and Ensure Original powder carries no third-party certification that we could find. Abbott's launch release describes the 3-in-1 as NSF Contents Certified, and the Abbott Store listing says it is NSF-certified and independently tested for quality and safety. We did not find the product in a search of NSF's public listings at the time of writing, which can lag new launches, so look for the NSF mark on the tub itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            NSF Contents Certified is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport. Contents certification checks that what is in the tub matches the label. Certified for Sport adds screening for substances banned in competitive sport and lot-by-lot testing, which is what tested athletes need. For most buyers, contents certification is a meaningful step up from nothing, because it means an independent lab has checked the protein and creatine claims. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> explains the difference between the marks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ensure Original powder is regulated and labeled as a food, with a Nutrition Facts panel, and is made by Abbott, one of the largest medical-nutrition manufacturers in the world. That brings food-safety manufacturing rules, but it is not independent certification. Abbott does not publish heavy-metal test results for the can, and we found no Clean Label Project, Informed Choice or NSF listing for it. That is typical for mainstream nutrition drinks rather than a red flag specific to Ensure.
          </p>

          <p>
            If contaminant testing is your main concern, the 3-in-1 is the better Ensure choice, and our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> covers what to look for on any label. For older adults taking several medications, the bigger safety questions are usually dose, sugar and kidney function rather than contamination, and those are covered further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
