          <p>
            For the Original powder, a 397 g can holds about seven 57 g servings, so around 63 g of protein at Abbott's 9 g figure. Prices we saw in November 2026 ran from $10.78 at Walmart to $12.29 at Target, $12.46 at H-E-B and $13.49 at Fred Meyer, which gives $4.28 to $5.35 per 25 g. If your can's label says 8 g per serving, the cost rises to roughly $4.80 to $6.00. Walmart's Equate Adult Nutrition Powder, a store-brand equivalent, was $7.96 a can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against ordinary protein powders, the 3-in-1 is mid-priced. Our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> puts that powder at about $1.20 to $1.70 per 25 g, and mainstream whey sits in a similar range. You pay a little more for Ensure's creatine, HMB and pharmacy-brand familiarity. You pay a lot more for the Original powder's protein, because you are really paying for calories and vitamins.
          </p>
